 "findings": [
  {
   "evidence": "Lines 15-20 and 34-38 of Mathematics/Calculus/Polynomial.lean; the eigenfunction equation proved downstream is `derivative ((1 - X ^ 2) * derivative (legendre n)) = -(n * (n + 1)) * legendre n` (Legendre/Basic.lean:250-252) and the orthogonality integral is unweighted (Legendre/Basic.lean:324).",
   "file": "EpsilonEridani/Mathematics/Calculus/Polynomial.lean",
   "fix": "In both places, call `1 - X ^ 2` the leading coefficient `p` of the Sturm\u2013Liouville operator and say it vanishes at `\u00b11`, which is why the boundary term drops on `[-1, 1]`; state that the orthogonality weight is 1. E.g. \"the Sturm\u2013Liouville symmetry for the operator with leading coefficient `1 - x ^ 2`, which vanishes at the endpoints `\u00b11`\". Drop \"weighted derivative operator\" at lines 15 and 34 accordingly.",
   "issue": "The module docstring calls `1 - x ^ 2` \"the weight\" of the Sturm\u2013Liouville problem (\"the Sturm\u2013Liouville symmetry for the weight `1 - x ^ 2` on `[-1, 1]`\"); the same wording repeats in the theorem docstring at line 37, and line 15/34 call the operator \"weighted\". In `(p y')' + q y = -\u03bb w y` for Legendre, `p = 1 - x\u00b2` and the weight is `w = 1`. The downstream result this file supports, `integral_legendre_mul_legendre`, is orthogonality with respect to Lebesgue measure (weight 1), not weight `1 - x\u00b2` (that would be Gegenbauer/Jacobi), so the wording can mislead a reader about the measure in the orthogonality relation.",
   "line": 18
  }
 ],
